# Managed by Ansible (roles/core_service) — local edits are overwritten.
#
# Binds the uploaded-file archive to the database dump, so the two halves of a backup are always
# taken together and always in the right order.
#
# **Why a drop-in rather than lines in the unit itself.** The unit belongs to roles/postgres, which
# runs BEFORE core_service in the Core play — and a role's defaults only come into scope for roles
# after it. `easy_files_backup_enabled` is core_service's, so a `{% raw %}{% if %}{% endraw %}` in postgres's template would
# read it as undefined and silently never emit these lines. A drop-in written by the role that owns
# the variable cannot fail that way, and it is installed only where the file archive exists.
#
# **Why this fixes the gap it was written for.** Every path that takes a pre-deploy dump starts this
# same unit: `deploy/deploy.sh` (PRE_RESTART_DUMP), `roles/core_rollout` via
# `core_rollout_dump_service`, `import-prod-dump.yml`, and the nightly timer. Before this, only the
# nightly one had a matching archive, so a dump taken before a deploy paired with the *previous*
# night's files and anything uploaded that day restored as a broken image. One dependency edge here
# covers all four callers at once, with no change to deploy.sh, the rollout, or the sudoers grant —
# systemd starts the dependency itself, so the grant still names one unit.
#
# `Wants=` and not `Requires=`: a file archive that fails must not cost us the database dump. The
# dump is the half that cannot be reconstructed from anywhere.
#
# `After=` is what makes the order files-then-database, which is the direction `TeacherUploadFile`
# already writes in — an object with no row is invisible junk the sweep collects, a row with no
# object is a broken image. The dump waits for the archive to finish.

[Unit]
Wants=easy-files-backup.service
After=easy-files-backup.service
